1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:02,200
The libertarians need not apply.

2
00:00:02,201 --> 00:00:05,640
The 21st century butcher has many faces.

3
00:00:05,641 --> 00:00:10,040
A grim reaper, a carcass processor, a meat cutter,

4
00:00:10,041 --> 00:00:13,340
a safety inspector, and a salesman.

5
00:00:13,341 --> 00:00:18,120
He turns livestock into steak, hamburger, and sausage.

6
00:00:18,121 --> 00:00:20,240
He even makes house calls.

7
00:00:20,241 --> 00:00:23,160
Now, The Butcher on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:42,380
Each year in the United States, massive feedlots schedule more than 135 million head of livestock

9
00:00:42,381 --> 00:00:45,180
for their appointments with the butcher.

10
00:00:48,340 --> 00:00:51,960
The lone multitasking butcher of old is nearly extinct.

11
00:00:53,180 --> 00:00:58,500
In his place is an army of high-speed cutters and bacteria control specialists

12
00:00:58,501 --> 00:01:00,400
at a mass production factory.

13
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:03,940
But in a world of prepackaged products,

14
00:01:04,520 --> 00:01:08,160
there's a growing movement to recapture the secrets of the old world butcher.

15
00:01:09,080 --> 00:01:10,900
A little over a pound and a half.

16
00:01:12,220 --> 00:01:15,940
At the swank Nick and Steph's Steakhouse in downtown Los Angeles,

17
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:20,160
patrons come to celebrate a forgotten art from the butcher's meat locker.

18
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:25,360
It's called dry-aged beef.

19
00:01:25,361 --> 00:01:31,460
Nick and Steps imports whole strip loins and ages them in a climate-controlled aging room

20
00:01:31,461 --> 00:01:35,120
for up to 21 days.

21
00:01:35,121 --> 00:01:39,180
What's happening is it promotes the enzymes within the beef to start breaking down the

22
00:01:39,181 --> 00:01:42,180
tendons and muscle fiber.

23
00:01:42,181 --> 00:01:46,280
In essence, what it's doing is reversing the process of the rigor mortis, so it's actually

24
00:01:46,281 --> 00:01:51,880
loosening up the meat and tenderizing it.

25
00:01:51,881 --> 00:01:57,740
Some dry-aged steaks sell for as high as $50 per 10-ounce cut.

26
00:01:57,741 --> 00:02:01,340
Their high cost is a direct result of shrinkage.

27
00:02:01,341 --> 00:02:08,120
As the beef ages, the outer surface decays and surface bacteria must be removed.

28
00:02:08,121 --> 00:02:11,260
Extracting the stripped steaks and ribeyes requires a skilled butcher.

29
00:02:11,261 --> 00:02:17,440
As you can see, it's completely broken down, high marbleization, completely tenderized,

30
00:02:17,441 --> 00:02:20,860
almost completely broken down.

31
00:02:20,861 --> 00:02:23,460
The modern steakhouse uses retro techniques

32
00:02:23,461 --> 00:02:25,900
to get the best flavored steak.

33
00:02:25,901 --> 00:02:28,300
But our ancient ancestors didn't have the luxury

34
00:02:28,301 --> 00:02:31,460
of waiting for their dinner to age.

35
00:02:31,461 --> 00:02:34,640
There was some point or juncture or phase

36
00:02:34,641 --> 00:02:38,620
that our ancestors passed through where they left the trees,

37
00:02:38,621 --> 00:02:41,120
began to walk upright, and perhaps one

38
00:02:41,121 --> 00:02:42,800
of the earliest things that they did

39
00:02:42,801 --> 00:02:47,380
was crack stones to get an edge so they could leave

40
00:02:47,381 --> 00:02:50,540
the shelter of the trees, run down onto the savanna,

41
00:02:50,541 --> 00:02:55,280
perhaps to scavenge meat from a lion kill.

42
00:02:55,281 --> 00:02:56,980
The latest field research indicates

43
00:02:56,981 --> 00:02:58,740
that our quest for animal protein

44
00:02:58,741 --> 00:03:02,060
goes back millions of years.

45
00:03:02,061 --> 00:03:07,960
Recent finds in East Africa have uncovered broken animal

46
00:03:07,961 --> 00:03:10,920
bones that have cut marks on them.

47
00:03:10,921 --> 00:03:14,540
And this is a strong indication that human ancestors back 2

48
00:03:14,541 --> 00:03:17,640
and 1 half million years ago were actually using stone tools

49
00:03:17,641 --> 00:03:23,940
to physically remove meat from these animal bones.

50
00:03:23,941 --> 00:03:26,600
Approximately 5,000 years ago, we

51
00:03:26,601 --> 00:03:29,280
learned to smelt naturally occurring copper and tin

52
00:03:29,281 --> 00:03:32,940
together to form the alloy known as bronze.

53
00:03:32,941 --> 00:03:35,300
Perhaps one of the foremost innovations

54
00:03:35,301 --> 00:03:38,660
in prehistoric butchery was the development

55
00:03:38,661 --> 00:03:40,980
of metal cutting tools.

56
00:03:40,981 --> 00:03:44,020
Slowly, butchering evolved from a function performed

57
00:03:44,021 --> 00:03:46,720
for families or small nomadic groups

58
00:03:46,721 --> 00:03:51,340
into a service conducted for larger populations.

59
00:03:51,341 --> 00:03:54,720
Public slaughterhouses date back to Roman times.

60
00:03:54,721 --> 00:03:58,240
In 300 BC, animals were butchered in the open air

61
00:03:58,241 --> 00:04:01,100
at the Forum in Rome.

62
00:04:01,101 --> 00:04:04,000
On special occasions, wealthy Romans sometimes

63
00:04:04,001 --> 00:04:07,480
ate such exotic meats as giraffe and tiny mice

64
00:04:07,481 --> 00:04:10,500
stuffed with pine nuts.

65
00:04:10,501 --> 00:04:13,420
The more unusual the food, the more the guests of the host

66
00:04:13,421 --> 00:04:16,340
were impressed.

67
00:04:16,341 --> 00:04:20,760
In the Middle Ages, butchers were restricted to certain neighborhoods in which to ply their trade.

68
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:26,140
This was due to the large amount of animal waste and blood.

69
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:31,780
They were usually dumped into the middle of the street that served as an open sewer.

70
00:04:34,680 --> 00:04:42,160
On the North American continent, the nomadic nature of the native tribes didn't allow for the technological evolution occurring elsewhere.

71
00:04:42,161 --> 00:04:50,700
Most Native Americans used stone tools to butcher animals.

72
00:04:51,900 --> 00:04:56,880
Fairly expedient stone tools, often just a sharp stone flake.

73
00:04:57,360 --> 00:05:03,120
This flake has long, extremely sharp edges on this side and this side,

74
00:05:03,340 --> 00:05:11,020
which would be just perfect for slicing meat off a bone or disarticulating animals.

75
00:05:11,021 --> 00:05:18,020
And this is so sharp you can actually shave with it.

76
00:05:18,021 --> 00:05:22,020
For the modern butcher,

77
00:05:22,021 --> 00:05:25,020
the knife is still the foundation for every job.

78
00:05:25,021 --> 00:05:29,020
And most butchers rely on just a couple of basic knives,

79
00:05:29,021 --> 00:05:34,020
the butcher or steak knife and the boning knife.

80
00:05:34,021 --> 00:05:37,020
One thing has remained constant since ancient times,

81
00:05:37,021 --> 00:05:40,020
the need for a sharp knife.

82
00:05:40,021 --> 00:05:43,020
One time-honored method of checking the sharpened edge

83
00:05:43,021 --> 00:05:45,020
is the paper test.

84
00:05:45,021 --> 00:05:48,020
Sharp knife is very, very important in this business.

85
00:05:48,021 --> 00:05:50,020
When your knife could do this,

86
00:05:50,021 --> 00:05:53,020
you know you have a real good edge on it.

87
00:05:53,021 --> 00:05:55,020
You could cut anything.

88
00:05:55,021 --> 00:05:57,020
If you look at the cutting edge of a knife

89
00:05:57,021 --> 00:05:59,020
under a magnifying glass,

90
00:05:59,021 --> 00:06:01,020
it'll look almost like a hacksaw blade

91
00:06:01,021 --> 00:06:03,020
with a series of teeth.

92
00:06:03,021 --> 00:06:07,020
When you use the knife and it starts to become dull,

93
00:06:07,021 --> 00:06:13,120
dull, the first thing that happens are these little teeth, or as we call them in the factory,

94
00:06:13,121 --> 00:06:13,821
feathers.

95
00:06:13,822 --> 00:06:19,920
These little feathers bend over to the left or the right side of the cutting edge,

96
00:06:20,080 --> 00:06:24,920
and your knife becomes dull, and you notice right away that it's not cutting the way it did

97
00:06:24,921 --> 00:06:27,661
when it was factory fresh.

98
00:06:27,662 --> 00:06:35,040
In that case, you use a butcher steel, and you go through a process of

99
00:06:35,041 --> 00:06:41,360
hand-stealing your blade where you draw the knife blade along a hardened steel rod, what

100
00:06:41,361 --> 00:06:47,260
happens is that these little feathers are stood up straight. They're realigned.

101
00:06:48,600 --> 00:06:52,620
While basic knife shapes haven't changed much in thousands of years,

102
00:06:53,360 --> 00:06:56,779
manufacturing methods are certainly different today.

103
00:06:56,780 --> 00:06:59,540
The Dexter Russell Company in Southbridge,

104
00:06:59,680 --> 00:07:04,440
Massachusetts is the largest manufacturer of professional cutlery in the United States.

105
00:07:05,040 --> 00:07:09,040
The process of making a butcher knife starts with raw steel.

106
00:07:09,041 --> 00:07:14,040
Steel is iron with carbon added,

107
00:07:14,041 --> 00:07:18,040
and when the percentage of carbon approaches 1%,

108
00:07:18,041 --> 00:07:22,040
you start to get steels that are good for making cutlery.

109
00:07:22,041 --> 00:07:26,040
We're looking for a steel that has, first of all,

110
00:07:26,041 --> 00:07:30,040
enough carbon in it so that we can heat treat it.

111
00:07:30,041 --> 00:07:34,040
At Dexter Russell, the knife blanks are heated to 50 degrees.

112
00:07:34,041 --> 00:07:38,040
are heated to 1,500 degrees, then rapidly cooled down

113
00:07:38,041 --> 00:07:41,040
in a specially formulated oil bath.

114
00:07:41,041 --> 00:07:44,040
To me, I always tell people the steel

115
00:07:44,041 --> 00:07:46,040
is the heart of the knife, but the heat treatment

116
00:07:46,041 --> 00:07:48,040
gives it its soul.

117
00:07:48,041 --> 00:07:53,040
The trick to heat treating is to elevate the temperature

118
00:07:53,041 --> 00:07:58,040
of the steel so that it changes its physical state.

119
00:07:58,041 --> 00:08:01,040
Subjecting the knife blank to rapid cooling

120
00:08:01,041 --> 00:08:05,040
leaves the blade in an extremely brittle state.

121
00:08:05,041 --> 00:08:07,980
So there's a final step to the heat treating process

122
00:08:07,981 --> 00:08:10,240
that's called tempering.

123
00:08:10,241 --> 00:08:15,060
Tempering gives steel its flexibility, its springiness.

124
00:08:15,061 --> 00:08:18,620
We do that by putting the carbon steel

125
00:08:18,621 --> 00:08:24,460
into a furnace at 550 degrees and soaking it

126
00:08:24,461 --> 00:08:27,040
at that temperature for two hours.

127
00:08:27,041 --> 00:08:29,040
The classic image of the butcher

128
00:08:29,041 --> 00:08:32,240
a formidable looking blade known as the cleaver.

129
00:08:32,340 --> 00:08:35,840
But 20th century technology made the cleaver obsolete.

130
00:08:35,940 --> 00:08:38,280
The cleaver was used much more because they probably

131
00:08:38,380 --> 00:08:39,580
didn't have a bandsaw.

132
00:08:39,680 --> 00:08:43,580
What they did, they used a steak knife to cut through the meat.

133
00:08:43,680 --> 00:08:46,900
Then they used the cleaver to cut off the bone.

134
00:08:49,500 --> 00:08:51,900
The electric bandsaw was introduced to the butcher

135
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:55,400
in the early 20th century.

136
00:08:55,500 --> 00:08:57,300
The saw was a welcome advancement

137
00:08:57,301 --> 00:09:01,800
in the multimillion-year evolution of the butcher's tools.

138
00:09:01,801 --> 00:09:04,300
But just decades before its introduction,

139
00:09:04,301 --> 00:09:08,300
forces at work in one Midwestern town would forever alter the role

140
00:09:08,301 --> 00:09:12,300
of the traditional butcher.

141
00:09:12,301 --> 00:09:16,300
Romans sometimes roasted a chicken, stuffed inside a duck,

142
00:09:16,301 --> 00:09:21,300
stuffed inside a goose, stuffed inside a pig, and finally,

143
00:09:21,301 --> 00:09:24,300
stuffed inside a cow.

144
00:09:24,301 --> 00:09:27,500
The Butcher will return on Modern Martins,

145
00:09:27,600 --> 00:09:29,600
here on the History Channel.

146
00:09:33,540 --> 00:09:35,740
At Huntington Meats in Los Angeles,

147
00:09:35,840 --> 00:09:37,840
the skills of the old-fashioned butcher

148
00:09:37,940 --> 00:09:40,140
are still in demand.

149
00:09:40,240 --> 00:09:41,860
This is a boneless chuck roast.

150
00:09:41,960 --> 00:09:44,460
I'm going to trim it and split it.

151
00:09:44,560 --> 00:09:46,960
Here, you can get whatever you'd like.

152
00:09:47,060 --> 00:09:50,800
In a supermarket, they tend to be price-conscious

153
00:09:50,900 --> 00:09:53,700
because they have to compete with other supermarkets.

154
00:09:53,701 --> 00:09:59,000
I don't really have to compete with anybody because of the quality that I have.

155
00:10:00,400 --> 00:10:05,440
For centuries, the traditional butcher has dispensed skillfully prepared cuts of meat

156
00:10:05,441 --> 00:10:07,820
and sound advice to willing customers.

157
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:12,700
Today's neighborhood butchers rarely slaughter the animal,

158
00:10:13,120 --> 00:10:17,880
but they remain experts in all phases of getting meat from the pasture to the table.

159
00:10:18,960 --> 00:10:22,540
He comes out, he talks to the customer, he knows what he's cut,

160
00:10:22,541 --> 00:10:24,340
He's proud of what he cut.

161
00:10:24,341 --> 00:10:25,540
He wraps it up.

162
00:10:25,541 --> 00:10:26,540
People take it home.

163
00:10:26,541 --> 00:10:28,940
He has more personal contact with that meat all the way

164
00:10:28,941 --> 00:10:31,680
through the complete job.

165
00:10:31,681 --> 00:10:34,080
These butchers are modern survivors

166
00:10:34,081 --> 00:10:35,880
whose predecessors crossed the Atlantic

167
00:10:35,881 --> 00:10:39,260
to help feed the newly formed American colonies.

168
00:10:39,261 --> 00:10:43,920
As settlements evolved along the East Coast,

169
00:10:43,921 --> 00:10:48,600
they would harvest wild game as well as domestic animals.

170
00:10:48,601 --> 00:10:51,840
And those products would be sold fresh

171
00:10:51,841 --> 00:10:56,560
if they were going to preserve them, they would use high amounts of salt, put that in barrels.

172
00:10:57,760 --> 00:11:02,960
In colonial New England, butchers used salted beef to build an important export business,

173
00:11:04,320 --> 00:11:06,340
trading meat for West Indies molasses.

174
00:11:07,800 --> 00:11:12,640
As the colonies grew, the retail butcher shop became a fixture in every community.

175
00:11:13,780 --> 00:11:17,300
Butchering was done on a very local basis because there's no refrigeration.

176
00:11:17,301 --> 00:11:24,920
and also because the sort of mass factory system had not yet been put in place.

177
00:11:25,480 --> 00:11:31,060
Most cities had major stockyards or markets where local butchers would buy the livestock

178
00:11:31,061 --> 00:11:34,680
and then take them off and slaughter them, and then the meat would be served fresh.

179
00:11:35,780 --> 00:11:41,380
But by the late 19th century, the American butcher was engulfed by the Industrial Revolution.

180
00:11:41,381 --> 00:11:48,080
large stockyards built for exhibiting and auctioning livestock began expanding in midwestern

181
00:11:48,081 --> 00:11:54,460
cities like cincinnati and kansas city but it was the railroads that ultimately ordained

182
00:11:54,461 --> 00:12:02,300
chicago illinois the meatpacking capital of america by the 1860s nearly every major railroad

183
00:12:02,301 --> 00:12:08,840
line passed through chicago making a large centralized stockyard a dominating economic

184
00:12:08,841 --> 00:12:13,240
By 1860-61, Chicago took the title from Cincinnati

185
00:12:13,340 --> 00:12:16,740
and became the pork butcher of the world.

186
00:12:16,840 --> 00:12:19,580
The endless pens and cattle runs encompassed

187
00:12:19,680 --> 00:12:24,180
more than a square mile of land on Chicago's south side.

188
00:12:24,280 --> 00:12:27,600
To process the multitude of beef cattle and hogs arriving

189
00:12:27,700 --> 00:12:31,100
by train, large packing houses were erected,

190
00:12:31,200 --> 00:12:33,640
some of them as tall as eight stories.

191
00:12:33,740 --> 00:12:37,440
The original packing houses were driven by gravity.

192
00:12:37,441 --> 00:12:40,440
that is, livestock would be taken to the tops of buildings.

193
00:12:40,441 --> 00:12:42,440
There they'd be slaughtered, and in their bodies

194
00:12:42,441 --> 00:12:45,440
would be carried down on an inclined plane

195
00:12:45,441 --> 00:12:47,440
to the various departments until they'd end up

196
00:12:47,441 --> 00:12:50,440
in the lower basements in the chill rooms.

197
00:12:50,441 --> 00:12:55,440
By the 1890s, meat packers employed overhead chain conveyors

198
00:12:55,441 --> 00:12:58,440
to transport the carcasses from the kill floor

199
00:12:58,441 --> 00:13:01,440
through a maze of specialized workers.

200
00:13:01,441 --> 00:13:03,440
Chicago packing houses also invented

201
00:13:03,441 --> 00:13:06,440
a huge byproducts business.

202
00:13:06,441 --> 00:13:12,380
From animal hides, bones, blood, and organs,

203
00:13:13,320 --> 00:13:15,660
packers created profitable markets for goods,

204
00:13:15,860 --> 00:13:19,940
including lard, fertilizer, glue, soap, and leather products.

205
00:13:21,400 --> 00:13:27,140
The number of cattle killed in Chicago went from 224,000 in 1875

206
00:13:27,141 --> 00:13:30,860
to more than 2.2 million in 1890.

207
00:13:32,540 --> 00:13:35,340
Les O'Rear took a job on the disassembly line

208
00:13:35,341 --> 00:13:37,620
at the Armour Packing House in 1932.

209
00:13:38,840 --> 00:13:42,020
And here's the squeal of the hogs,

210
00:13:42,740 --> 00:13:46,020
and the heat of the day, and the animal heat,

211
00:13:46,860 --> 00:13:50,360
and there's a great noise going on,

212
00:13:50,700 --> 00:13:55,160
but there's this inevitable movement of the conveyor belt.

213
00:13:55,820 --> 00:13:58,140
Never stops, never stops, never stops.

214
00:13:58,300 --> 00:14:00,960
Ten and twelve-hour days were not uncommon at all.

215
00:14:01,640 --> 00:14:03,560
In fact, ten was sort of normal.

216
00:14:03,561 --> 00:14:06,100
You work Saturdays too, you know.

217
00:14:06,600 --> 00:14:08,860
Maybe it was a half day off for Saturday.

218
00:14:09,560 --> 00:14:15,420
And you doesn't miss your cut because then that will stop the line.

219
00:14:15,700 --> 00:14:19,040
And there's hell to pay if you stop the line.

220
00:14:20,200 --> 00:14:24,320
In Chicago's famous packing houses, the butcher's job was fragmented.

221
00:14:25,820 --> 00:14:28,760
Well, the butcher, of course, became one of 200 men.

222
00:14:28,761 --> 00:14:34,040
You know, the original job of a butcher was to slaughter and dress the beef.

223
00:14:34,580 --> 00:14:36,060
These were very skilled men.

224
00:14:36,740 --> 00:14:40,780
But if you were going to make money in slaughtering large numbers of animals,

225
00:14:40,860 --> 00:14:45,060
you cut the jobs down so that unskilled workers at the turn of the century

226
00:14:45,061 --> 00:14:46,900
were getting about 14 cents an hour.

227
00:14:48,420 --> 00:14:52,720
The men who were very skilled were getting about 50 cents an hour.

228
00:14:54,340 --> 00:14:57,080
Chicago's packing town rose to dominance in part

229
00:14:57,081 --> 00:14:59,720
because of the development of one major technology,

230
00:15:00,680 --> 00:15:01,920
refrigeration for shipping.

231
00:15:03,120 --> 00:15:06,240
For many years, natural refrigeration using ice

232
00:15:06,241 --> 00:15:10,060
cut from frozen ponds preserved fresh meat for weeks on end.

233
00:15:11,020 --> 00:15:12,320
The problem was shipping it.

234
00:15:13,480 --> 00:15:18,100
In 1875, a Massachusetts cattle dealer named Gustavus Swift

235
00:15:18,101 --> 00:15:20,100
opened a packing house in Chicago.

236
00:15:21,740 --> 00:15:24,040
Knowing the potentially huge profits

237
00:15:24,041 --> 00:15:26,160
of shipping fresh meat to eastern markets,

238
00:15:26,161 --> 00:15:29,120
he perfected the first refrigerated rail car.

239
00:15:30,440 --> 00:15:34,140
These custom cars were loaded with block ice at the slaughterhouse,

240
00:15:34,440 --> 00:15:37,100
then reloaded at various stops along the route.

241
00:15:39,080 --> 00:15:42,280
Swift's experiment created the market for Chicago dress feed,

242
00:15:43,340 --> 00:15:46,960
a term that described a fully processed carcass delivered fresh,

243
00:15:47,500 --> 00:15:48,260
hanging on a hook.

244
00:15:49,340 --> 00:15:52,520
The first meat that Swift brought to the eastern markets,

245
00:15:52,521 --> 00:15:56,140
local butchers who were making money slaughtering their own cattle,

246
00:15:56,620 --> 00:15:59,440
refused to handle it and called it embalmed meat.

247
00:15:59,640 --> 00:16:02,340
There was an awful lot of bad publicity about this.

248
00:16:02,420 --> 00:16:04,700
There was this talk of Western embalmed meat

249
00:16:04,701 --> 00:16:08,660
and Chicago selling sick beef on the eastern markets.

250
00:16:08,960 --> 00:16:09,940
But the beef was cheaper.

251
00:16:10,600 --> 00:16:12,820
In fact, Swift basically said to his agents,

252
00:16:13,520 --> 00:16:15,900
make it as cheap even if you have to give it away

253
00:16:15,901 --> 00:16:17,680
so you can get a foot in the market.

254
00:16:17,900 --> 00:16:18,600
And he did.

255
00:16:19,360 --> 00:16:22,440
In 1906, author Upton Sinclair

256
00:16:22,441 --> 00:16:24,780
called The Jungle.

257
00:16:24,880 --> 00:16:27,480
Sinclair hoped to draw attention to the miserable working

258
00:16:27,580 --> 00:16:29,940
conditions of the packing houses.

259
00:16:30,040 --> 00:16:32,680
But the book had a very different impact.

260
00:16:32,780 --> 00:16:36,680
There's 13, maybe 15 pages of meat processing in the book.

261
00:16:36,780 --> 00:16:39,960
And those 13 or 15 pages of meat processing

262
00:16:40,060 --> 00:16:42,100
is what really struck the public.

263
00:16:42,200 --> 00:16:45,260
But it had a tremendous impact because it hit them right in the

264
00:16:45,360 --> 00:16:46,660
stomach, you know.

265
00:16:46,760 --> 00:16:49,060
This is what I'm eating, you know, this kind of stuff.

266
00:16:49,160 --> 00:16:52,440
And he's talking about men falling to vats of lava.

267
00:16:52,441 --> 00:16:54,480
He's talking about fingers being chopped off

268
00:16:54,580 --> 00:16:57,180
and going out as a hash.

269
00:16:57,280 --> 00:17:00,680
He's, you know, all this kind of stuff.

270
00:17:00,780 --> 00:17:03,080
The resulting outcry helped bring about the Meat

271
00:17:03,180 --> 00:17:07,680
Inspection Act of 1906 and the Pure Food and Drug Act.

272
00:17:07,780 --> 00:17:11,600
Both passed within months of the book's publication.

273
00:17:11,700 --> 00:17:15,700
Together, the laws brought huge changes.

274
00:17:15,800 --> 00:17:17,540
All animals had to pass an inspection

275
00:17:17,640 --> 00:17:21,740
by the newly formed United States Drug Administration.

276
00:17:21,741 --> 00:17:24,700
All carcasses were subject to post-mortem inspection,

277
00:17:25,340 --> 00:17:28,920
and cleanliness standards were established for all phases of the job.

278
00:17:30,860 --> 00:17:37,140
For the next 75 years, Swift, Armor, and other Chicago meatpacking giants

279
00:17:37,141 --> 00:17:38,740
controlled much of the industry.

280
00:17:39,680 --> 00:17:43,640
But as sure as transportation technology built the Union stockyards,

281
00:17:44,160 --> 00:17:47,280
its further revolution also tore them down.

282
00:17:48,240 --> 00:17:51,460
Because as they become no longer dependent on the railroad,

283
00:17:51,740 --> 00:17:54,860
That is neither for bringing their livestock in or for shipping their meat out.

284
00:17:55,220 --> 00:17:58,980
You have refrigerator trucks that are moving along the new highway systems.

285
00:17:59,700 --> 00:18:02,680
And you get this massive decentralization of the industry.

286
00:18:02,681 --> 00:18:06,960
Today, the only remaining evidence of the world's largest meatpacking enterprise

287
00:18:06,961 --> 00:18:10,780
is the original Stonegate on Chicago's south side.

288
00:18:12,420 --> 00:18:19,000
After 106 years of continuous operation, Union stockyards officially closed in 1971.

289
00:18:19,001 --> 00:18:23,000
But the packing house has evolved into highly automated

290
00:18:23,100 --> 00:18:26,300
regional processing plants, where the final product is no

291
00:18:26,400 --> 00:18:29,940
longer a side of beef, but a box of beef.

292
00:18:30,040 --> 00:18:32,340
Today, the traditional butcher's livelihood

293
00:18:32,440 --> 00:18:34,740
is threatened by the fast-cutting, pre-packaging

294
00:18:34,840 --> 00:18:37,360
skills of a mass production army.

295
00:18:42,300 --> 00:18:45,200
A popular treat in St. Louis in Chicago at the height

296
00:18:45,300 --> 00:18:49,000
of the stockyard era was the brain sandwich.

297
00:18:49,001 --> 00:18:54,500
deep-fried slices of cow brains served with onions between two slices of bread.

298
00:18:56,040 --> 00:18:59,420
The Butcher will return on Modern Marvels.

299
00:19:03,980 --> 00:19:08,020
Chicago was once the center of America's massive meatpacking industry.

300
00:19:09,760 --> 00:19:13,500
Today, Chicago's legacy is scattered across the Great Plains

301
00:19:13,501 --> 00:19:16,860
in states like Minnesota, Nebraska, and Iowa.

302
00:19:16,861 --> 00:19:25,680
Feed lots bigger than many of the local towns now provide the animals for a business worth more than $80 billion annually.

303
00:19:27,660 --> 00:19:30,765
According to the American Meat Institute, U.S.

304
00:19:30,766 --> 00:19:34,680
production of red meat exceeded 38 billion pounds in 1990.

305
00:19:36,360 --> 00:19:40,620
The butcher's craft is now a sophisticated alliance of man and machine,

306
00:19:41,180 --> 00:19:45,640
a high-speed mass production disassembly of traditional meat products,

307
00:19:45,641 --> 00:19:50,520
and a staggering assortment of processed meats and inedible byproducts.

308
00:19:50,521 --> 00:19:57,400
The industry is very efficient in terms of how they utilize all the parts from an animal.

309
00:19:57,700 --> 00:19:58,700
There's very little waste.

310
00:19:59,960 --> 00:20:02,180
It all starts with steam cleaning,

311
00:20:02,760 --> 00:20:06,260
a process that removes dirt and other contaminants from the hide.

312
00:20:07,500 --> 00:20:10,960
The animal's hide is removed by a mechanical hide puller,

313
00:20:10,961 --> 00:20:16,480
And the 1,300-pound carcass is halved using massive back splitter saws.

314
00:20:17,140 --> 00:20:21,020
The large sides of beef are kept in cold storage for a number of days.

315
00:20:22,940 --> 00:20:27,140
This allows the enzymes to break down the meat before cutting begins.

316
00:20:30,340 --> 00:20:37,340
On its journey to the night, each carcass is graded for quality and yield by highly trained USDA graders.

317
00:20:37,341 --> 00:20:42,340
The graders examine the ribeye portion for the dispersion,

318
00:20:42,440 --> 00:20:44,340
or marbling, of fat cells.

319
00:20:46,340 --> 00:20:48,880
Select beef has slight marbling.

320
00:20:48,980 --> 00:20:51,780
Choice offers modest marbling.

321
00:20:51,880 --> 00:20:55,100
And prime-grade beef contains the best dispersion of fat

322
00:20:55,200 --> 00:20:58,300
and fetches the highest market price.

323
00:20:58,400 --> 00:21:01,800
The marbled fat is what gives the cooked steak much of its flavor.

324
00:21:01,801 --> 00:21:02,501
of its flavor.

325
00:21:06,300 --> 00:21:09,200
In recent years, the human eye is being replaced

326
00:21:09,201 --> 00:21:13,600
by a video grading system in many plants.

327
00:21:13,601 --> 00:21:17,200
This imaging device calculates color and marbling of fat

328
00:21:17,201 --> 00:21:19,100
and assigns a quality grade in seconds.

329
00:21:21,900 --> 00:21:25,380
Prior to cutting, many processors inject nitrogen

330
00:21:25,381 --> 00:21:28,400
or other gases into the carcass to help separate muscle

331
00:21:28,401 --> 00:21:31,400
from bone and make disassembly easier.

332
00:21:31,401 --> 00:21:35,840
We have a carcass coming in, and we will take certain parts off,

333
00:21:35,900 --> 00:21:37,240
and they will go down one line.

334
00:21:37,400 --> 00:21:39,260
Another set of parts will go down another line.

335
00:21:39,261 --> 00:21:44,300
We'll have people that are specialized in, say, pulling out a strip loin

336
00:21:44,301 --> 00:21:47,740
and then cutting it into steaks, for example.

337
00:21:48,320 --> 00:21:52,120
A modern meat processing facility is much like any industrial workplace.

338
00:21:52,940 --> 00:21:54,320
Safety is a major concern.

339
00:21:55,620 --> 00:21:58,420
Because of repetitive motion and the speed of the lines,

340
00:21:58,421 --> 00:22:04,300
the mass production butcher wears a vest and gloves of chain mail to avoid potential accidents

341
00:22:04,301 --> 00:22:06,942
with the razor-sharp blades.

342
00:22:06,943 --> 00:22:12,980
A vast majority of the butchered beef cuts are primals, or large

343
00:22:12,981 --> 00:22:19,660
portions, such as the sirloin or the chuck, that will be further processed into retail cuts.

344
00:22:22,140 --> 00:22:27,240
In large processing facilities, all of the large primal beef cuts are sent to the packaging

345
00:22:27,241 --> 00:22:28,009
department.

346
00:22:28,010 --> 00:22:34,020
The first vacuum packaging technologies were intended to remove air from around a meat

347
00:22:34,021 --> 00:22:34,721
product.

348
00:22:34,722 --> 00:22:39,960
The lack of oxygen inhibits bacteria and helps to preserve a sterile environment.

349
00:22:42,680 --> 00:22:47,860
Today, the vastly improved vacuum packaging technology is critically important to butchers

350
00:22:47,861 --> 00:22:54,240
and consumers alike. We're seeing more and more products being vacuum packaged,

351
00:22:54,241 --> 00:22:57,280
consumer-ready products that are very convenient.

352
00:22:57,820 --> 00:23:02,640
And it ranges from luncheon meats and cured products,

353
00:23:02,740 --> 00:23:04,620
things like that, because it has the ability

354
00:23:04,621 --> 00:23:06,740
to maintain the color of those products.

355
00:23:07,500 --> 00:23:10,300
At the University of Illinois' Meat Sciences Lab,

356
00:23:11,360 --> 00:23:13,280
researchers are looking at ways to improve

357
00:23:13,281 --> 00:23:16,040
upon a much newer technology known as MAP,

358
00:23:16,520 --> 00:23:18,500
or Modified Atmosphere Packaging,

359
00:23:19,020 --> 00:23:22,280
a process of modifying the atmosphere around the meat

360
00:23:22,281 --> 00:23:24,320
by injecting a mixture of gases.

361
00:23:25,960 --> 00:23:30,220
And one of the thought processes was to increase the shelf life

362
00:23:30,221 --> 00:23:35,420
of fresh meat in the retail store was to add oxygen to the gas mix

363
00:23:35,421 --> 00:23:38,640
so that the meat cut looked like it was a bright cherry red

364
00:23:38,641 --> 00:23:40,680
or to give it its natural color.

365
00:23:42,020 --> 00:23:45,620
University of Illinois researchers also study an industry movement

366
00:23:45,621 --> 00:23:46,840
called enhancement.

367
00:23:48,800 --> 00:23:51,740
Enhancement is a process in which meat products are injected

368
00:23:51,741 --> 00:23:54,600
with various solutions to improve palatability.

369
00:23:55,520 --> 00:23:59,240
Typically, you will find salt, phosphate.

370
00:24:00,300 --> 00:24:03,100
Sometimes they'll use a natural flavoring,

371
00:24:03,240 --> 00:24:06,500
like rosemary that has some antioxidant characteristics.

372
00:24:07,320 --> 00:24:11,840
And many products will contain either sodium or potassium lactate.

373
00:24:11,980 --> 00:24:15,200
What it does is obviously adds a little bit of flavor,

374
00:24:15,201 --> 00:24:17,960
a little saltiness to it, which enhances the meat flavor.

375
00:24:17,961 --> 00:24:24,160
But maybe more importantly, it changes some of the characteristics of what's called the protein matrix,

376
00:24:24,840 --> 00:24:28,960
and it opens that matrix and allows for more water-protein interactions,

377
00:24:30,020 --> 00:24:35,300
and that also allows the meat to become more tender once it's cooked.

378
00:24:36,600 --> 00:24:41,940
Researchers experimenting with different enhancements use both mechanical and human taste testers.

379
00:24:41,941 --> 00:24:48,640
using an instron device shear force is used to measure the tenderness of various cooked

380
00:24:48,641 --> 00:24:56,440
enhanced steaks consumer taste panels are also used to measure the palatability of different

381
00:24:56,441 --> 00:25:04,000
injected solutions from these blind taste tests researchers can gauge what new recipes will

382
00:25:04,001 --> 00:25:10,620
appeal to the mass market meat enhancement for many chicken beef and pork products is a well

383
00:25:10,621 --> 00:25:13,720
established practice.

384
00:25:13,721 --> 00:25:15,820
But what about the meat that the butcher trims

385
00:25:15,821 --> 00:25:18,220
from the primal cuts?

386
00:25:18,221 --> 00:25:21,840
Trimmings are a key component of the all-American cookout,

387
00:25:21,841 --> 00:25:23,960
hamburgers and hot dogs.

388
00:25:23,961 --> 00:25:28,840
Hamburger is a process where we're going to take cuts of

389
00:25:28,841 --> 00:25:32,180
meat, lean meat, and we typically will add what is

390
00:25:32,181 --> 00:25:33,180
known as trim.

391
00:25:33,181 --> 00:25:38,240
And so these are higher fat content parts of the animal.

392
00:25:38,241 --> 00:25:47,200
And we will blend them in a specific proportion to give us, say, an 80-20, a 90-10 lean-to-fat ratio.

393
00:25:49,420 --> 00:25:54,040
Some extra lean hamburger contains even less than 10% fat.

394
00:25:57,580 --> 00:26:01,260
But hot dogs have a fat content of more than 20%,

395
00:26:01,261 --> 00:26:03,183
although that doesn't stop U.S.

396
00:26:03,184 --> 00:26:06,780
consumers from eating more than 1 million tons every year.

397
00:26:08,240 --> 00:26:14,240
These beef or pork products are made with an odd assortment of animal parts.

398
00:26:14,241 --> 00:26:20,240
There are federal guidelines for the amount of meat and or byproducts that go into a hot

399
00:26:20,241 --> 00:26:24,240
dog or a sausage.

400
00:26:24,241 --> 00:26:29,240
The labeling is such that it will tell you, for example, a beef hot dog has to obviously

401
00:26:29,241 --> 00:26:30,240
have beef in it.

402
00:26:30,241 --> 00:26:32,240
It's certainly a wholesome product.

403
00:26:32,241 --> 00:26:37,240
It's not something that you may want to see on your dinner table in its original form,

404
00:26:37,241 --> 00:26:48,560
But generally when I say byproducts, maybe some liver or organ meat or parts of a pig that you ordinarily wouldn't want to eat.

405
00:26:48,960 --> 00:26:52,380
For example, cheek meat or, you know, snouts or whatever.

406
00:26:55,600 --> 00:26:58,340
Sausage is the oldest processed meat product.

407
00:26:58,940 --> 00:27:05,340
For thousands of years, we've used salt and animal intestines to preserve and encase meat for later consumption.

408
00:27:07,240 --> 00:27:11,960
By the 11th century, European butchers began specializing in sausage making,

409
00:27:12,520 --> 00:27:18,040
and it soon became a highly regarded art, with recipes passed down from generation to generation.

410
00:27:20,120 --> 00:27:23,560
Today, most sausage making is performed by the mega processors.

411
00:27:26,280 --> 00:27:31,800
High-speed production lines allow the industry to produce over 1 million tons of hot dogs every year.

412
00:27:31,801 --> 00:27:39,840
But not all producers pump out millions of pounds every week.

413
00:27:39,841 --> 00:27:44,800
Some family-run producers focus on quality rather than quantity.

414
00:27:44,801 --> 00:27:49,780
At Eusinger's in Milwaukee, Wisconsin, old world tradition meets a modern yet limited

415
00:27:49,781 --> 00:27:51,780
production facility.

416
00:27:51,781 --> 00:27:57,800
We employ about 100 people and we are basically a very large butcher shop, more so than a

417
00:27:57,801 --> 00:28:00,980
highly automated facility.

418
00:28:00,981 --> 00:28:05,300
We try and keep our recipes as authentic as they were three generations ago.

419
00:28:05,840 --> 00:28:10,940
So that means we don't use a lot of processing aids such as monosodium glutamate.

420
00:28:11,400 --> 00:28:14,500
The onions we use are fresh onions. We use fresh garlic.

421
00:28:15,600 --> 00:28:21,000
At Eusinger's, meat, water, and fresh spices are mixed and combined in a grinding machine

422
00:28:21,001 --> 00:28:23,180
to create a unique sausage emulsion.

423
00:28:24,720 --> 00:28:30,340
In manufacturing a fresh sausage, a refrigerant such as dry ice is often added

424
00:28:30,341 --> 00:28:36,820
during the chopping portion, and that is to keep the meat very, very cold during chopping,

425
00:28:36,821 --> 00:28:39,924
because the chopping produces friction, which produces heat.

426
00:28:39,925 --> 00:28:41,580
But it's very important that the

427
00:28:41,581 --> 00:28:47,460
meat stays cold during chopping, so that you have particle definition between the fat and the lean,

428
00:28:47,461 --> 00:28:54,000
because in fresh sausage, the product has to look its best because site sells.

429
00:28:55,000 --> 00:28:57,740
The emulsion is then put through a stuffing machine.

430
00:28:58,840 --> 00:29:03,600
Here, processed edible animal intestines act as the casings for the sausages.

431
00:29:05,860 --> 00:29:10,740
Eusinger's sausages are hung on racks and smoked in a smoking chamber for a period of days,

432
00:29:11,980 --> 00:29:16,000
completed to the unique specifications of three generations of sausage makers.

433
00:29:16,001 --> 00:29:21,060
When you're making specialty, you're trying to differentiate yourself from the mass-produced product,

434
00:29:21,061 --> 00:29:26,580
And I think that the American consumer is awakening to that fact and being a more adventurous eater.

435
00:29:27,560 --> 00:29:33,520
But specialty meat processors share one very grave concern with the large mass production butchers.

436
00:29:35,180 --> 00:29:41,840
It's a terrifying new threat that cannot be seen or tasted, but still has the power to kill.

437
00:29:44,600 --> 00:29:49,000
Cow eyes are sometimes sold by meatpacking operations to medical research centers

438
00:29:49,001 --> 00:29:51,780
and also to parts of Europe and the Middle East,

439
00:29:52,120 --> 00:29:54,080
where they are considered delicacies.

440
00:29:55,220 --> 00:29:57,960
The Butcher will return on Modern Marvels.

441
00:30:04,680 --> 00:30:07,120
Despite major advances in technology

442
00:30:07,121 --> 00:30:08,560
throughout the 20th century,

443
00:30:09,100 --> 00:30:11,380
the trend toward mass production of meat

444
00:30:11,381 --> 00:30:13,300
created the potential for new threats

445
00:30:13,301 --> 00:30:14,740
to the expanding population.

446
00:30:15,800 --> 00:30:17,600
As the corner butcher gave way

447
00:30:17,601 --> 00:30:22,460
to industrialized meatpacking, the chance of spreading an outbreak of disease over a

448
00:30:22,461 --> 00:30:25,485
wide area increased dramatically.

449
00:30:25,486 --> 00:30:30,160
The most commonly recognized foodborne bacteria are

450
00:30:30,161 --> 00:30:34,553
Campylobacter, Salmonella, and E. coli.

451
00:30:34,554 --> 00:30:39,960
Up until the 1990s, the USDA regularly inspected

452
00:30:39,961 --> 00:30:44,145
all slaughtering and processing facilities in the United States.

453
00:30:44,146 --> 00:30:45,780
However, a major outbreak

454
00:30:45,781 --> 00:30:46,481
of E.

455
00:30:46,482 --> 00:30:53,280
coli in 1994 of more than 500 confirmed infections caused a re-examination of sanitary

456
00:30:53,281 --> 00:30:55,052
guidelines and practices.

457
00:30:55,053 --> 00:30:59,660
In the mid-90s, meat processors began adopting a set of practices

458
00:30:59,661 --> 00:31:06,737
known as HACCP. HACCP stands for Hazard Analysis Critical Control Points.

459
00:31:06,738 --> 00:31:09,160
The system was originally

460
00:31:09,161 --> 00:31:14,220
developed in the 1950s for NASA to ensure the safety of food in the space program.

461
00:31:14,221 --> 00:31:18,460
And HACCP is a process by which the manufacturer

462
00:31:18,560 --> 00:31:23,420
or the processor is involved in providing safety

463
00:31:23,520 --> 00:31:24,960
for his products.

464
00:31:25,060 --> 00:31:27,760
He designs a plan to assure the safety,

465
00:31:27,860 --> 00:31:31,280
and then basically the government supervises or oversees

466
00:31:31,380 --> 00:31:35,280
that he's following the plan that he's developed.

467
00:31:35,380 --> 00:31:37,780
HACCP consists of seven steps, which

468
00:31:37,880 --> 00:31:40,020
can be applied to any butchering operation

469
00:31:40,120 --> 00:31:42,820
to ensure food safety.

470
00:31:42,821 --> 00:31:49,580
Under USDA guidelines, every meat processing facility in the United States has to implement

471
00:31:49,581 --> 00:31:51,537
and follow a HACCP plan.

472
00:31:51,538 --> 00:31:57,080
The Great Western Beef Company is a portion control house, receiving

473
00:31:57,081 --> 00:32:02,300
large primals from the packing houses and cutting them into high-end steaks for hotels

474
00:32:02,301 --> 00:32:03,533
and restaurants.

475
00:32:03,534 --> 00:32:09,160
Great Western rigorously monitors their plan, following HACCP guidelines.

476
00:32:09,161 --> 00:32:14,040
When we start first thing in the morning, I do a pre-op sanitation.

477
00:32:14,340 --> 00:32:22,140
We check all the equipment, tables, cutting boards, lugs, carts, hand knives, utensils, etc.

478
00:32:23,140 --> 00:32:25,200
Check for cleanliness of the equipment.

479
00:32:26,240 --> 00:32:33,120
At Great Western, processing area room temperature is taken three times a day to ensure a 40-degree environment.

480
00:32:34,160 --> 00:32:37,020
Meat products are sampled for temperatures every 30 minutes.

481
00:32:37,021 --> 00:32:40,920
and each day random samples are trimmed from packed boxes

482
00:32:40,921 --> 00:32:44,200
and sent to a USDA lab for pathogen testing.

483
00:32:45,920 --> 00:32:48,560
Since the government has started testing for E. coli,

484
00:32:48,660 --> 00:32:49,900
it has dropped dramatically.

485
00:32:50,240 --> 00:32:52,040
Products that I have coming in-house,

486
00:32:52,041 --> 00:32:54,360
I have never tested positive for E. coli.

487
00:32:56,000 --> 00:32:58,580
Each night after the meat cutters finish their work,

488
00:32:59,060 --> 00:33:00,400
the cleanup crew takes over.

489
00:33:00,680 --> 00:33:05,760
They use 180-degree water, they rinse, foam everything down.

490
00:33:07,020 --> 00:33:14,940
Hand scrub all the equipment, rinse again, then they apply a sanitizer, either a quaternary or a chlorinated sanitizer.

491
00:33:15,340 --> 00:33:18,320
We alternate to ensure the effectiveness of both.

492
00:33:18,820 --> 00:33:25,420
In response to demands for food safety, large volume meat processors have adopted many new technologies.

493
00:33:27,120 --> 00:33:35,320
The automatic hide washing cabinet treats cattle hides with a high-powered antibacterial spray to remove dirt and bacterial contaminants.

494
00:33:37,020 --> 00:33:40,600
High-pressure steam vacuums remove excess fluids.

495
00:33:41,840 --> 00:33:47,860
Next, a dilute organic acid wash similar to vinegar kills any residual pathogens.

496
00:33:48,640 --> 00:33:54,080
Before the rapid cooling process, workers check each carcass with a Verify system,

497
00:33:54,900 --> 00:33:59,440
a powerful blacklight that can detect any stray specks of organic contaminants.

498
00:34:01,340 --> 00:34:05,740
Workers continually trim product samples and swab work surfaces.

499
00:34:07,020 --> 00:34:14,020
An on-site lab facility cultures these samples and checks for any bacterial growth.

500
00:34:14,021 --> 00:34:18,020
Every butcher needs to be especially careful with ground beef and hamburger products,

501
00:34:18,021 --> 00:34:25,020
as they tend to have a much higher incidence of foodborne illness than muscle cuts of meat.

502
00:34:25,021 --> 00:34:31,020
One of the reasons that hamburger sometimes is suspect or gets singled out more commonly

503
00:34:31,021 --> 00:34:36,020
as a suspect in, say, foodborne illness outbreaks, it's a mixed product.

504
00:34:36,021 --> 00:34:37,580
It's not a whole mussel product anymore.

505
00:34:38,260 --> 00:34:41,440
And if you think about a whole mussel product, say a steak,

506
00:34:41,540 --> 00:34:44,700
that intact mussel is, in essence, sterile.

507
00:34:45,400 --> 00:34:48,300
And the only place that it can be contaminated would be on its surface.

508
00:34:48,960 --> 00:34:51,940
Hamburger, on the other hand, because it's a mixed product,

509
00:34:52,060 --> 00:34:56,300
if there were any bacteria on the surface of those original meat products that were ground,

510
00:34:56,301 --> 00:34:58,640
they're now in the inside of that product.

511
00:34:59,940 --> 00:35:02,560
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention

512
00:35:02,561 --> 00:35:06,898
recently announced significant declines in illnesses caused by E.

513
00:35:06,899 --> 00:35:09,300
coli, salmonella, and campylobacter.

514
00:35:10,800 --> 00:35:17,120
But even as sanitation has improved, a new menace has emerged that threatens the worldwide beef supply.

515
00:35:18,260 --> 00:35:20,130
In December 2003, the U.S.

516
00:35:20,131 --> 00:35:25,380
Department of Agriculture announced that an adult cow in Washington state

517
00:35:25,381 --> 00:35:29,380
tested positive for bovine spongiform encephalopathy,

518
00:35:30,320 --> 00:35:33,600
also known as BSE, or mad cow disease.

519
00:35:35,000 --> 00:35:36,640
BSE is one of those deals.

520
00:35:38,020 --> 00:35:41,320
It was not a matter of if it would get here,

521
00:35:41,500 --> 00:35:42,540
it's when it would get here.

522
00:35:43,100 --> 00:35:45,520
And it didn't take long for it to finally get here.

523
00:35:46,900 --> 00:35:49,000
BSE is a degenerative disease

524
00:35:49,001 --> 00:35:51,500
that attacks the central nervous systems of cattle,

525
00:35:52,220 --> 00:35:55,340
destroying brain tissue, and eventually causing death.

526
00:35:55,380 --> 00:36:02,380
Many scientists believe the disease is caused by a malformed protein that has been named a prion.

527
00:36:02,381 --> 00:36:10,380
Prions multiply by converting normal protein cells into abnormally shaped cells that eventually clog the entire brain.

528
00:36:10,381 --> 00:36:16,380
These misshapen proteins are infectious, but cannot be killed by cooking or freezing.

529
00:36:16,381 --> 00:36:23,380
Scientists believe the disease has spread among cattle through so-called animal recycling.

530
00:36:23,381 --> 00:36:28,860
recycling, the use of cattle parts in commercial cattle feed.

531
00:36:28,861 --> 00:36:31,880
Nearly 100 people have died from a human variant of mad cow

532
00:36:31,881 --> 00:36:36,800
disease, probably from eating infected beef products.

533
00:36:36,801 --> 00:36:39,320
The disease is carried in nervous system tissue,

534
00:36:39,321 --> 00:36:42,160
like the spinal cord, and is most likely found

535
00:36:42,161 --> 00:36:45,920
in products like sausage.

536
00:36:45,921 --> 00:36:49,480
Early symptoms in humans include insomnia, memory loss,

537
00:36:49,481 --> 00:36:51,000
and dementia.

538
00:36:51,001 --> 00:36:54,800
Currently, there is no known cure for this fatal disease.

539
00:36:54,801 --> 00:36:57,100
In the US, the Food Safety Inspection Service

540
00:36:57,101 --> 00:36:59,880
has taken numerous steps to ensure the safety

541
00:36:59,881 --> 00:37:02,320
of the beef supply.

542
00:37:02,321 --> 00:37:06,340
It's something we all have to watch out for.

543
00:37:06,341 --> 00:37:08,240
The laws and rules and regulations

544
00:37:08,241 --> 00:37:11,400
since that the first one was found out in Washington

545
00:37:11,401 --> 00:37:14,740
has changed a lot of the way we handle a lot of beef

546
00:37:14,741 --> 00:37:15,460
comes in here.

547
00:37:15,461 --> 00:37:20,300
So without a doubt, we will improve the safety and the

548
00:37:20,400 --> 00:37:22,760
wholesomeness of the food products that are coming to

549
00:37:22,860 --> 00:37:24,200
our table.

550
00:37:24,300 --> 00:37:27,200
I feel very comfortable in saying we probably have the

551
00:37:27,300 --> 00:37:31,840
safest food supply in the United States of anywhere in the world.

552
00:37:31,940 --> 00:37:35,120
Experts agree it will take a very concerted and focused

553
00:37:35,220 --> 00:37:37,980
effort on the part of both large and small butchering

554
00:37:38,080 --> 00:37:42,380
operations to eliminate the threat of mad cow disease.

555
00:37:42,381 --> 00:37:46,220
As a safeguard against mad cow disease,

556
00:37:46,221 --> 00:37:48,380
the skulls, brains, vertebral columns,

557
00:37:48,381 --> 00:37:52,380
and spinal cords of cattle 30 months of age or older

558
00:37:52,381 --> 00:37:56,380
are prohibited in the US food supply.

559
00:37:56,381 --> 00:37:59,380
The butcher will return on Modern Marvels,

560
00:37:59,381 --> 00:38:01,380
here on the History Channel.

561
00:38:06,380 --> 00:38:08,380
The traditional butcher is a survivor

562
00:38:08,381 --> 00:38:09,380
in a changing business.

563
00:38:09,381 --> 00:38:11,380
When I was a boy, I was a kid.

564
00:38:11,381 --> 00:38:16,380
When I was a boy in the 1950s and in the early 1960s,

565
00:38:16,381 --> 00:38:18,380
I lived in an inner city community

566
00:38:18,381 --> 00:38:21,380
which had maybe 10 butcher shops in a walking distance.

567
00:38:21,381 --> 00:38:25,380
Those butcher shops would have sawdust on the floor,

568
00:38:25,381 --> 00:38:27,380
and it was a place where people met.

569
00:38:27,381 --> 00:38:28,380
That has shifted.

570
00:38:28,381 --> 00:38:30,380
Now you go to the supermarket,

571
00:38:30,381 --> 00:38:34,380
and you pick something out in a plastic container that is wrapped,

572
00:38:34,381 --> 00:38:37,380
and it may be frozen, it may not be frozen,

573
00:38:37,381 --> 00:38:40,380
but it certainly isn't processed in front of you.

574
00:38:40,381 --> 00:38:47,800
Today's factory-cut, vacuum-packaged meat products have forced the traditional butcher to seek out specialized niches in the meat marketplace.

575
00:38:49,520 --> 00:38:58,520
Chicago's Polina Market is a classic specialty meat market for urban consumers, determined to maintain the tradition of face-to-face contact with the butcher.

576
00:38:59,120 --> 00:39:08,900
When you go to that supermarket or superstore, you're looking at a lot of things that have been pre-cut, not by the butcher shop, not by the people working there.

577
00:39:08,901 --> 00:39:13,740
Boxed beef came along, and now they're into centralized cutting.

578
00:39:13,940 --> 00:39:17,900
Centralized cutting has cut out the...

579
00:39:17,901 --> 00:39:19,420
the... us.

580
00:39:19,680 --> 00:39:21,120
We start with the whole cattle.

581
00:39:21,340 --> 00:39:22,300
We work our way down.

582
00:39:22,400 --> 00:39:23,240
We trim it.

583
00:39:23,560 --> 00:39:25,240
We make all the portion cuts.

584
00:39:25,820 --> 00:39:29,100
And when a customer comes in here, they can actually talk to a butcher

585
00:39:29,101 --> 00:39:33,000
who can tell you what to do with that and how to cook it and how to prepare it.

586
00:39:34,180 --> 00:39:38,380
Another type of niche butcher is unknown to millions of city-dwelling consumers.

587
00:39:38,900 --> 00:39:42,900
This country butcher sidesteps the large packing houses and

588
00:39:43,000 --> 00:39:45,900
deals directly with a family farmer.

589
00:39:46,000 --> 00:39:47,900
Eichmann's Meats in Seward, Illinois,

590
00:39:48,000 --> 00:39:49,900
is the small farmer's slaughterhouse.

591
00:39:50,000 --> 00:39:51,900
We are what they call a custom processor.

592
00:39:52,000 --> 00:39:53,900
It's almost like being a custom tailor.

593
00:39:54,000 --> 00:39:56,900
Local farmers will bring in a beef to us.

594
00:39:57,000 --> 00:39:57,900
We'll slaughter them.

595
00:39:58,000 --> 00:40:00,900
We'll hang them and age them for about two weeks.

596
00:40:01,000 --> 00:40:03,900
Then we bring them out, cut them as to their specifications.

597
00:40:04,000 --> 00:40:07,900
And then we process it, freeze it, give the customer a quick

598
00:40:07,901 --> 00:40:11,820
give the customer a call, they come in, pick them up, and take it home for their use.

599
00:40:13,420 --> 00:40:17,340
Sometimes delivering livestock from the ranch to the slaughterhouse isn't practical.

600
00:40:19,020 --> 00:40:25,100
In cases like this, another niche butcher steps up. He is called the mobile butcher.

601
00:40:25,660 --> 00:40:27,020
My job description is

602
00:40:30,060 --> 00:40:36,314
A to Z. You know, we go out, slaughter the animals. So I'm in there doing that.

603
00:40:36,315 --> 00:40:37,660
I'm also in there

604
00:40:37,661 --> 00:40:41,640
cutting and wrapping the animals, processing it for the people the way they like it.

605
00:40:42,800 --> 00:40:46,320
Getting to the farms on Washington State's tiny Lumi Island

606
00:40:46,321 --> 00:40:48,520
means taking the ferry from the mainland.

607
00:40:50,180 --> 00:40:53,580
The 28-foot mobile processing unit, or MPU,

608
00:40:55,240 --> 00:40:58,160
contains everything the mobile butcher needs for his long day.

609
00:40:59,840 --> 00:41:06,160
Typically, the customer with this USDA mobile unit

610
00:41:06,161 --> 00:41:09,920
has the opportunity to have us come right to their farm,

611
00:41:10,920 --> 00:41:13,540
slaughter their animals under inspection,

612
00:41:14,480 --> 00:41:17,160
therefore allowing them to further retail their meat.

613
00:41:18,460 --> 00:41:22,300
The day begins by leveling the MPU on the uneven ground

614
00:41:22,301 --> 00:41:25,320
to ensure that fluids will drain in the proper direction.

615
00:41:27,640 --> 00:41:29,900
Next, the sanitation process begins.

616
00:41:31,020 --> 00:41:32,800
We have two different sanitizers.

617
00:41:32,940 --> 00:41:35,140
One's for organic and one's for non-organic.

618
00:41:35,141 --> 00:41:38,280
And this is the quaternary, which is non-organic,

619
00:41:38,281 --> 00:41:42,140
and we have an iodine sanitizer for organic, so...

620
00:41:42,141 --> 00:41:45,140
We spray all the surfaces just to kill any bacteria

621
00:41:45,141 --> 00:41:47,140
that might be present.

622
00:41:47,141 --> 00:41:50,140
With a clean environment, the mobile butcher turns his

623
00:41:50,141 --> 00:41:52,140
attention to the dirty work.

624
00:41:52,141 --> 00:41:58,140
This is one of the stun guns, which, if you can get close

625
00:41:58,141 --> 00:42:03,140
enough to the animal, we can use one of these.

626
00:42:03,141 --> 00:42:10,140
And it just has shells like this that you pop in the end of it.

627
00:42:10,141 --> 00:42:16,140
And there's a pin in there which will stun the animal.

628
00:42:16,141 --> 00:42:20,140
Now we've got to find the animals.

629
00:42:20,141 --> 00:42:24,140
When an animal can't be restrained for a short-range job,

630
00:42:24,141 --> 00:42:27,140
the butcher uses a single bullet.

631
00:42:33,140 --> 00:42:47,140
Just as his predecessors did for thousands of years, the mobile butcher removes the hide

632
00:42:52,080 --> 00:42:55,900
and processes the animal without assistance.

633
00:42:55,901 --> 00:43:02,680
Despite the remote location, an on-site USDA inspector oversees the day's work.

634
00:43:02,681 --> 00:43:06,760
variety of knives and electric saws, a single steer is dressed

635
00:43:06,761 --> 00:43:13,320
in approximately two hours. At the end of the day, the

636
00:43:13,321 --> 00:43:16,760
butcher cleans and sanitizes tools and the MPU before

637
00:43:16,761 --> 00:43:20,440
departing. He will later turn the animals

638
00:43:20,441 --> 00:43:23,400
into cuts of meat for the farmer's freezer.

639
00:43:23,401 --> 00:43:25,400
You know, they're getting what they want.

640
00:43:25,401 --> 00:43:27,640
They don't have to go to the store and just say, well, that's

641
00:43:27,641 --> 00:43:31,080
what I've got to buy. No, they can actually create

642
00:43:31,081 --> 00:43:35,580
different cuts, different styles of cuts.

643
00:43:35,680 --> 00:43:38,020
The processing plants have forever changed the world

644
00:43:38,120 --> 00:43:41,520
of the traditional retail butcher.

645
00:43:41,620 --> 00:43:45,220
Some wonder if that world can continue to survive.

646
00:43:45,320 --> 00:43:48,000
Are we a dying breed?

647
00:43:48,100 --> 00:43:50,640
Yeah, I really think so.

648
00:43:50,740 --> 00:43:52,440
At one time in the city of Chicago,

649
00:43:52,540 --> 00:43:55,500
there was a tavern and a butcher shop on every block.

650
00:43:55,600 --> 00:43:56,900
That's gone.

651
00:43:57,000 --> 00:43:58,040
There's a few of us that have survived,

652
00:43:58,041 --> 00:44:06,280
And we're here to supply those that appreciate that difference.

653
00:44:06,380 --> 00:44:09,580
It's the deft skill with a knife, the intimate knowledge

654
00:44:09,680 --> 00:44:12,480
of the product, and the secrets handed down

655
00:44:12,580 --> 00:44:16,400
through generations that may ultimately save the traditional

656
00:44:16,500 --> 00:44:18,900
butcher from extinction.

657
00:44:19,000 --> 00:44:21,400
As long as there's a demand for quality,

658
00:44:21,500 --> 00:44:22,400
there will be a butcher.

